markdown <<MARKDOWN
<div style="font-size:14pt; line-height:1.4;">

  <h2 style="font-size:14pt; margin:0 0 8px 0;">Level 1: A clearer way to write Promise code</h2>

  <p style="margin:0 0 10px 0;">
    You just learned how to use a Promise chain with <code>.then()</code> to handle asynchronous code (like <code>fetch</code>).
    Now you’ll learn a different way to write the <em>same</em> logic so it reads more like normal, step-by-step code:
    <strong>async</strong> and <strong>await</strong>.
  </p>

  <hr style="margin:10px 0;" />

  <p style="margin:0 0 6px 0;"><strong>Key vocabulary</strong></p>

  <ul style="margin:0 0 10px 20px; padding:0;">
    <li style="margin:0 0 6px 0;">
      <code>async</code> marks a function as asynchronous. An <code>async</code> function automatically returns a
      <strong>Promise</strong>, and it allows you to use <code>await</code> inside it.
    </li>
    <li style="margin:0 0 6px 0;">
      <code>await</code> is used inside an <code>async</code> function. It pauses <em>that function</em> until a Promise resolves,
      then gives you the resolved value so the next line can run.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p style="margin:0 0 10px 0;">
    <strong>Important:</strong> async/await does <em>not</em> make the code “less asynchronous.”
    It’s a cleaner way to write Promise logic you already understand.
  </p>

  <hr style="margin:10px 0;" />

  <p style="margin:0 0 6px 0;"><strong>Quick Check (answer in your own words)</strong></p>
  <ol style="margin:0 0 0 20px; padding:0;">
    <li style="margin:0 0 6px 0;">Where are you allowed to use <code>await</code>?</li>
    <li style="margin:0 0 6px 0;">Does <code>await</code> pause the whole program, or just one function?</li>
  </ol>

</div>

MARKDOWN
